WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.900
Static and long-lived objects can look simple because their storage lasts for a long time.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.200
That does not make them automatically safe or maintainable.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:19.340
A long lifetime removes one category of scope problem, but it introduces questions about access order, initialization assumptions, shared mutation, testing, and shutdown behavior.

4
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.100
Global objects can hide dependencies.

5
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:28.040
A function may appear independent while reading or modifying state that lives elsewhere.

6
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:36.300
This makes code harder to test and harder to review because the lifetime of the object is long, but the valid-use rules are still distributed across the program.

7
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.460
Static locals preserve state between calls.

8
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:45.240
That can be useful for caches or counters, but it may affect reentrancy and repeated use.

9
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:52.940
A function that seems pure or isolated may behave differently on the second call because the static object retained previous state.

10
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:59.700
Thread-local storage can help avoid some cross-thread sharing, but it has its own lifetime and initialization rules.

11
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:08.200
Pointers to thread-local objects should not escape into contexts that can outlive the thread or use the pointer from another thread without a clear contract.

12
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.040
Shared mutable state needs synchronization and ownership discipline.

13
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:22.820
A long-lived object may still be invalid for a particular operation if another part of the program is modifying it, shutting it down, or resetting it.

14
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.800
Shutdown behavior should be explicit rather than based on guesses about module order.
